const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What is the actual purpose of career counseling?',
    a: 'Its purpose is to close the gap between what you actually know about yourself and what the job market genuinely rewards, using structured self-assessment, real market information, and a repeatable decision process - not to hand you a single "correct" career.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is the purpose of career counseling the same as its benefits?',
    a: 'No. Purpose is about what problem the practice exists to solve - closing the self-knowledge-to-market gap. Benefits are about what a person personally gains from doing it, such as saved time, avoided wrong turns, or more confidence. Purpose explains the "why it exists"; benefits explain the "what you get."',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does career counseling exist as a formal practice at all?',
    a: 'It was formalised in the early 1900s because industrialisation created far more job categories than any one person, family, or school could evaluate alone. Frank Parsons built a structured method - know yourself, know the field, reason between the two - specifically to solve that information gap, and the core purpose has not changed since.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does career counseling only serve people who are confused or stuck?',
    a: 'No. Its purpose applies at any decision point with real uncertainty - choosing a stream after school, picking a first job among multiple offers, deciding whether to switch fields mid-career, or re-entering work after a break. Confusion is one common trigger, not the only one.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can career counseling actually tell me the one right career for me?',
    a: 'No credible counselor claims that, and treating any assessment result as a single verdict misunderstands what the tools are for. The honest purpose is narrowing a wide field down to a short, evidence-backed list and giving you a way to test it - not naming one final answer.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is the purpose of career counseling different from a career aptitude test?',
    a: 'An aptitude or interest test produces one piece of evidence about fit. Career counseling uses that evidence as one input inside a larger process that also checks market demand, weighs personal constraints, and builds a decision you can repeat later - the test is a tool inside the purpose, not the purpose itself.',
  },
